  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM EDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nWith observing platforms showing temperatures having already dropped \ninto the 30s across much of the interior U.P. early this morning, \nand with some spots already getting as low as the mid-20s, a \nwidespread frost/freeze is being realized this morning as high \npressure ridging skirting overhead is creating mostly clear skies. \nTherefore, the current Freeze Warning for all but Keweenaw County \n(and the Frost Advisories over the far east) will expire at 10 AM \nEDT this morning as it will take a couple of hours of sunshine to \nwarm things up at the sfc. With a Clipper still slated to move \ntowards the area today, expect cloud cover to gradually increase \nfrom west to east throughout the daylight hours. This, in \nconjunction with the antecedent cooler air aloft, will keep highs \nmainly in the mid to upper 50s, although some spots may be able to \nsneak to or just above 60. While some guidance like the NAM suite \nhas rainfall starting as early as late this morning to early this \nafternoon over the west half, thinking the antecedent dry air in the \nlower levels may take more time to be overcome due to the sfc \nridging moving overhead early this morning (that escapes to the east \nlater today). That being said, as the atmospheric profile moistens \nin the lower levels by the mid to late afternoon hours from west to \neast across the Yoop, expect to see light rainfall falling across \nmost of the area by the time the sun sets this evening. While not \nmuch rainfall is expected with the Clipper's passage, thankfully the \nrainfall amounts have trended ever-so-slightly upwards, with most of \nthe area seeing a 30 to 60% chance of seeing more than a quarter \ninch of rainfall between this evening and Sunday evening. However, \nrainfall amounts could be higher over the higher terrain of \nMarquette County as the latest REFS shows chances of over a quarter \ninch of rainfall at around 60 to 85%, whereas the 'Golden Triangle' \nregion from Iron Mountain, Escanaba, and Menominee only as a 25% \nchance or less of that happening (city of Menominee near 0%). \n\nWhile we can expect some breezy conditions today with winds \nincreasing from the south to 20 to 25 mph by this evening, expect \ngustier northwest winds behind the Clipper low on Sunday; northwest \nwind gusts of 30 to even occasionally 40 mph could be seen over the \nKeweenaw and along the Lake Superior shoreline east of Marquette \nbehind the low. While the delta-Ts and strong winds over Lake \nSuperior may support a very quick burst of post-frontpa light rain \nshowers on Sunday, lake effect rain/drizzle shower chances don't \nappear to impact the area until Sunday night as another round of \nreinforcing CAA drops down into the region. The incoming Clipper and \nCAA behind it should keep temperatures fairly normal the rest of \ntoday through Monday, with highs generally being in the 50s to lower \n60s. \n\nMoving into the rest of next week, expect a slight warm up by the \nmid-week as another Clipper system passing through northern Ontario \ndraws in a little bit of WAA. Thus, expect highs to return to the \n60s with some spots getting up to around 70 by Tuesday and \nWednesday. However, with guidance mostly keeping the area dry during \nthe mid-week with the Clipper's passage, this may lead to fire \nweather concerns given the drought conditions across the area, the \nstrong winds ahead of and behind the Clipper's passage, and the \nfalling dead leaves offering another sfc fuel source for fire to \nwork with. With the jet stream staying nearly overhead as we head \ninto late next week, we could see temperatures return to near normal \nagain before rising above normal yet again to end the period.",
